---
export const prerender = true;
import EditorialArticleLayout from '../../components/EditorialArticleLayout.astro';

const slug = 'open-source-projects-behind-access-free-tools';
const title = '8 Open-Source Projects Behind Access Free Tools';
const description =
  'See which projects build Access Free Tools, test its results, and power browser OCR and AI, plus the limits of its optional cloud question router.';
const imagePath = '/social/open-source-projects-behind-access-free-tools.webp';
const socialImagePath = '/social/open-source-projects-behind-access-free-tools.png';
const imageAlt =
  'Full-body smoke-kawaii girl at a workbench arranging eight colorful open-source building blocks beside a laptop showing a utility webpage.';
const sources = [
  { href: 'https://astro.build/blog/astro-7/', label: 'Astro 7 release' },
  { href: 'https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/node/', label: 'Astro Node adapter' },
  { href: 'https://react.dev/', label: 'React documentation' },
  { href: 'https://devblogs.microsoft.com/typescript/announcing-typescript-7-0/', label: 'Microsoft: Announcing TypeScript 7.0' },
  { href: 'https://playwright.dev/', label: 'Playwright documentation' },
  { href: 'https://playwright.dev/docs/accessibility-testing', label: 'Playwright: accessibility testing limits' },
  { href: 'https://vitest.dev/', label: 'Vitest documentation' },
  { href: 'https://github.com/naptha/tesseract.js', label: 'Tesseract.js on GitHub' },
  { href: 'https://tesseract-ocr.github.io/tessdoc/ImproveQuality.html', label: 'Tesseract: image quality limits' },
  { href: 'https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers.js/index', label: 'Transformers.js documentation' },
  { href: 'https://github.com/ollama/ollama', label: 'Ollama on GitHub' },
  { href: 'https://docs.ollama.com/api/introduction', label: 'Ollama local and cloud API routes' },
  { href: 'https://ladybird.org/', label: 'Ladybird browser project' },
  { href: 'https://lightpanda.io/', label: 'Lightpanda browser project' },
  { href: 'https://github.com/open-webui/open-webui', label: 'Open WebUI on GitHub' },
  { href: 'https://www.continue.dev/', label: 'Continue project status' },
];
const relatedLinks = [
  {
    href: '/blog/free-ai-skills-open-source-tools-organic-growth/',
    label: 'AI skills and their limits',
    description: 'Compare AI skills, software projects, licenses, and possible provider costs.',
  },
  {
    href: '/categories/ai-tools/',
    label: 'Browser AI tools',
    description: 'Find OCR, text, keyword, language, and image helpers.',
  },
  {
    href: '/tools/image-to-text-ocr-tool/',
    label: 'Image to Text OCR Tool',
    description: 'Try the Tesseract.js workflow on a screenshot or photo.',
  },
  {
    href: '/why-access-free-tools/',
    label: 'Why Access Free Tools exists',
    description: 'The practical rules behind this growing free utility library.',
  },
];
---

<EditorialArticleLayout
  slug={slug}
  title={title}
  seoTitle="8 Open-Source Projects Behind Access Free Tools"
  description={description}
  breadcrumbLabel="Open-source projects behind the site"
  imagePath={imagePath}
  socialImagePath={socialImagePath}
  imageAlt={imageAlt}
  imageCaption="The smoke-kawaii mascot sorts eight projects used for the website, tests, browser OCR and AI, and optional cloud question routing."
  sources={sources}
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      A list of software names does not explain what a website does with them. On Access Free
      Tools, one project builds a calculator page, another checks the answer, and another reads
      text from an image. Here is what the eight projects do, which parts run in the browser,
      and where a cloud service enters the picture.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Quick answer</h2>
    <p>
      Astro builds the pages. React runs interactive interfaces. TypeScript checks code types.
      Playwright and Vitest test selected browser flows and logic. Tesseract.js and Transformers.js
      power specific OCR and AI tools. The Ask route can call Ollama's hosted API when its parser
      cannot identify a supported request.
    </p>
    <p>
      These are different jobs. A passing type check does not prove a formula is correct, and a
      successful browser test does not prove that every visitor can use a page. The stack gives
      the project repeatable checks, with limits that still need human judgment.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>What is actually used, and where</h2>
    <p>
      The dependency lockfile reviewed on October 5, 2026 contains Astro 7.2.8, React 19.2.8,
      TypeScript 7.0.2 and 6.0.2, Playwright 1.62.0, Vitest 4.1.11, Tesseract.js 7.0.0, and
      Transformers.js 4.2.0. Ollama is a separate hosted API connection, not a model bundled into
      the website. These version numbers describe this project snapshot, not the latest release
      of every upstream project.
    </p>
    <div class="editorial-table-shell" role="region" aria-label="Open-source project roles" tabindex="0">
      <table class="editorial-comparison-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Job</th><th>Projects</th><th>What they do here</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Build and interface</td><td>Astro, React, TypeScript</td><td>Render pages, run interactive tools, and check code types.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Quality checks</td><td>Playwright, Vitest</td><td>Check selected layouts, accessibility rules, interactions, and calculation logic.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>AI and OCR</td><td>Tesseract.js, Transformers.js, Ollama</td><td>Recognize images and run browser models; optionally send Ask questions through a cloud router.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Astro 7 builds content and server routes</h2>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Sources: <a href="https://astro.build/blog/astro-7/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Astro 7</a>
      and the <a href="https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/node/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Node adapter</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      Access Free Tools has hundreds of calculator, converter, guide, category, gallery, sitemap,
      and API routes. Astro renders the content as HTML and supports server routes for functions
      such as Ask. This project uses the Node adapter in standalone mode and requires Node 24.
      The October 5 published build marker also reported Node 24.
    </p>
    <p>
      The title, explanation, links, and examples exist in rendered HTML. A crawler does not need
      to press a button to read those parts. Sitemap and canonical rules still need separate
      checks. Rendering HTML does not promise search visibility or approval by an advertising network.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>React 19 runs the parts people touch</h2>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Source: <a href="https://react.dev/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">React documentation</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      The page around a calculator can stay plain HTML, while React handles the inputs, modes,
      results, undo buttons, and live status inside the tool. Someone can read the formula and
      example before JavaScript finishes loading, then use the interactive part when it is ready.
    </p>
    <p>
      The project checks JavaScript budgets and loads larger OCR and model assets only for the
      matching tool action. That keeps the work associated with the feature that needs it. A
      mortgage calculation does not need a speech model to produce its answer.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>TypeScript 7 checks types; examples check the math</h2>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Source: <a href="https://devblogs.microsoft.com/typescript/announcing-typescript-7-0/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Microsoft's TypeScript 7 announcement</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      A calculator result can look believable even when one field is wired to the wrong unit.
      TypeScript can catch mismatched types and missing cases before code reaches the browser.
      The project uses TypeScript 7's native command-line checker and keeps the TypeScript 6
      compatibility package. Microsoft documents this dual-package setup; the project's scripts
      offer both <code>tsc</code> and <code>tsc6</code> checks.
    </p>
    <p>
      Types cannot prove an interest formula uses the right assumption. Result tests must also
      check known inputs and outputs. For example, 15% of 80 must give 12 whether the request comes
      from the percentage calculator or its deterministic API runner.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Playwright checks browser flows; Vitest checks logic</h2>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Sources: <a href="https://playwright.dev/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Playwright</a>
      and <a href="https://vitest.dev/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Vitest</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      Vitest checks small units of logic, such as a legal Four in a Row move, a routing decision,
      or an indexation rule. Playwright opens pages and exercises interactions. It can detect
      layout problems that a calculation test would never see.
    </p>
    <p>
      Layout checks at different widths can catch overlaps, clipped tables, and horizontal
      overflow. They cover the browser and states tested, rather than every phone or assistive
      technology. Playwright's <a href="https://playwright.dev/docs/accessibility-testing" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">accessibility guidance</a>
      recommends manual assessment and inclusive user testing alongside automated checks.
      An axe scan cannot replace those steps.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Tesseract.js and Transformers.js serve specific tools</h2>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Sources: <a href="https://github.com/naptha/tesseract.js" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Tesseract.js</a>
      and <a href="https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers.js/index" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Transformers.js</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a href="/tools/image-to-text-ocr-tool/">Image to Text OCR Tool</a> starts a self-hosted
      Tesseract.js worker, core, and selected language data when someone presses Read text.
      Transformers.js is imported when a matching browser model is requested. Downloading a model
      is separate from uploading a user's input for inference.
    </p>
    <p>
      Tesseract's <a href="https://tesseract-ocr.github.io/tessdoc/ImproveQuality.html" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">image-quality guide</a>
      explains why resolution, contrast, skew, and borders affect recognition. Check names, totals,
      dates, and anything important against the original. The <a href="/blog/browser-ai-vs-local-ai-privacy/">browser AI privacy guide</a>
      explains the separate model-download, input-processing, and analytics routes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Ollama provides optional cloud routing, not calculator math</h2>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Sources: <a href="https://github.com/ollama/ollama" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Ollama on GitHub</a>
      and its <a href="https://docs.ollama.com/api/introduction" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">API routes</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/ask/">Ask Access Free Tools</a> sends a visitor's question to the site's server.
      Its parser tries to identify a supported tool first. If that fails and cloud routing is
      configured, the server can send the question to <code>https://ollama.com/api/chat</code>.
      This is cloud AI, not a model running on the visitor's computer.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ollama's job is to select a tool and its inputs. The final arithmetic and answer come from
      the deterministic runner. A recognized parser question does not need the Ollama call;
      an unrecognized question may fail if that service is unavailable. Using an open-source
      project through a hosted service does not make the service local or automatically free.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Other projects are not production dependencies</h2>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Sources: <a href="https://ladybird.org/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Ladybird</a>,
      <a href="https://lightpanda.io/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Lightpanda</a>, and
      <a href="https://github.com/open-webui/open-webui" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Open WebUI</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      Ladybird is building an independent browser engine. Lightpanda targets headless automation.
      Open WebUI provides a self-hosted interface for local and cloud model workflows. They offer
      useful comparisons, but none appears as a production dependency in this site's reviewed
      package files. Their presence in a roundup does not show that they power this website.
    </p>
    <p>
      Continue's <a href="https://www.continue.dev/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">official site</a>
      says it was acquired by Cursor, as checked on October 5, 2026. Ownership, maintenance,
      licensing, and hosting can change. Before adding any project, check its current source and
      the exact job it would do. The <a href="/blog/how-to-check-github-project-before-installing/">GitHub project checklist</a>
      covers that next step.
    </p>
  </section>
</EditorialArticleLayout>
